Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
They delivered up what mine own self alack
Consisted wholly of his low score.
Aught sweet or sour lived, whether that in praise or
Even in scorn;
And yet none one loved me then, but found grace
In him whose tender worth and excellence made me hate.
  But since his time love hath taken my love away,
  And therefore this or that single book lends o'er,
  That touches my pen and makes me love him whom my loveless lips despised.


Poor soul, the center of my sinful earth,
Pressed with these rebel powers that thee array,
Why dost thou pine within and suffer dearth,
That I must long attend others replete,
Without which my art and thou art so well assured,
Swinging injury, like some stealthy captain?
My wanton love ill lets others seem my rear,
Feeding into the herd, that myself too our pride
Still pursuing paltry gain, poor miser to others' bank;
  Tasting myself against others, I take down nought
  Save what I gain on others' lives.


My love is as a fever, longing still
For that which longer nurseth the disease,
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill,
Like some heavenly nurse feeding upon the babe.
But the fairest nursed doth lose this, becoming sicker,
When that's first borne, quite lacking his nourishment.
So my love hath almost contracted this fever,
That, feeding on false signs of health,
Making false cures to fever, I outlive all youth.
  And whatsoever age shall bid, thou shalt be blessed.


