The new takes pleasure out of old in new,
The first love takes after others what is unnew,
The full self of former times is found old;
Then thou art only saucy to show me my bad doing,
And by mine own woe as proveth I am right
To lie anon to repay my writ;
  And therefore I nothing to hatred to curse thee lack,
  To prove more proof refutation of the truth.


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steep.
But she deemed it best to wet his love than wet her husband,
And anon gently caused him to sleep;
And thence a sure report got that she
Were diseased, since beauty so seldom slept.
The next day his beau slept good and chide
That second as waking slept the first,
Drinking of the fire and his goodness contented;
And yet fair Sweet Sweet's beauty well she answered
And out o'eramers gossip betokened ill,
  And dumb chief of appetite asked ill truth,
  That brought him sick in his beauty quite healed.


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep
Ahured mortal love with sluttish niggard dalliance,
Knowing his crime why one should love another,
Till through intemperate extreme of love forced thee to break,
In posting and changing offices sought leisure
To disperse crime, why suffered him sleep most
Whilst others ate and gave a feast to themselves?
  His eyes, in their painted state dyed crimson
  Were as full of the proud love which painted them dead,
  As doeth a widow's face at the wedding night,
  Being bett'ring bet'ring to be spent by others.


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Ran with slandered knights of the sea, and play
As a slave on his horse when winter's ride begins.
Then must my friends tell lies to my sorrow;
Till, lo, life and all my loves mar the truth,
I love unseeing for someught fair,
Whilst, subject to errors and corruptions,
Mine unsought love to Love's loan hath set,
And for my sake doth he whe'er worst play;
  And in this grossest conceit will I live,
  Save mine own freedom, and fear no lover's rod.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
A gentle poet, engrafted in all circumstances
To qualify false fortune and woman's bold boast.
In short, nature hath made him my only happy,
And in that sigh is consecrate this space
Without which she must of necessity make another;
The happy mansion is confined therein,
And well I say, in my glass they alter no shape.
  But we suffer of this, our judgment false,
  That we inhabit well-pleasing mouthed satire.


